he problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nTwo bicycle racers cross the starting line on a circular track at 12:15 PM. One cyclist takes $12$ minutes per lap, while the other completes a lap every $16$ minutes. Assuming their speeds remain constant, what is the next time they cross the starting line together?  Your answer should be in the form $h:m_1m_2,$ like 3:45.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cc2ab6a38160d2b20794c86d5fcf761991a1a27804e4cad930d72451018b0c8d"},"seconds":18.57745122909546,"seed":967934355599960871,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the next time the two bicycle racers cross the starting line together, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their lap times.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the lap times.**\n- The first cyclist completes a lap every 12 minutes.\n- The second cyclist completes a lap every 16 minutes.\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the LCM of 12 and 16.**\n- First, find the prime factorizations:\n  \\[\n  12 = 2^2 \\times 3\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  16 = 2^4\n  \\]\n\n- The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in these factorizations:\n  \\[\n  \\text{LCM} = 2^4 \\times 3 = 16 \\times 3 = 48\n  \\]\n\n**Step 3: Determine the next time they meet.**\n- They started at 12:15 PM.\n- The LCM of their lap times is 48 minutes.\n\n- Add 48 minutes to 12:15 PM:\n  \\[\n  12:15 \\text{ PM} + 48 \\text{ minutes} = 1:03 \\text{ PM}\n  \\]\n\nThus, the next time they cross the starting line together is \\(\\boxed{1:03}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1:03}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
